 1.2 We justify extension to $k$ without assuming an unproved radical base-change rule. For any finite-dimensional split algebra $T$ over $k_0$, choose representatives $S_1,\ldots,S_r$ of its simple modules; there are finitely many because every simple is a quotient of the regular module and thus a factor of a composition series of $T$. The ideal $N=\bigcap_j\operatorname{Ann}_T(S_j)$ is nilpotent: it annihilates each factor of such a series, so its successive powers lower the series and $N^{\dim T}=0$. The action map $T\to\bigoplus_j\operatorname{End}_{k_0}(S_j)$ is surjective. Indeed choose bases in the $S_j$ and consider the image of $T$ in the direct sum of the corresponding tuples. If this image were proper, semisimplicity of the direct sum of simples would give a nonzero map to some $S_j$ killing the image. Maps between distinct simple modules are zero, and $\operatorname{End}_T(S_j)=k_0$, so this would yield a nontrivial scalar relation among the selected basis vectors of $S_j$ by evaluation at $1$. This is impossible. Thus $T/N$ is the displayed product of full matrix algebras. Under extension to $k$, the ideal $k\otimes N$ stays nilpotent, hence annihilates every simple: if it acted surjectively on a simple, nilpotence would contradict its nonzeroness. The quotient is still a product of full matrix algebras. It follows that all simples are exactly the extensions of the $S_j$, and extending a composition series preserves its simple factors and multiplicities. For completeness, a finite direct sum of simple modules is semisimple by induction: split a submodule's intersection with the previous summands, quotient that intersection, and project to the final simple summand. The projection is zero or onto; in the latter case its remaining submodule is the graph of a homomorphism from that simple into the complementary previous summands, and that graph has those previous summands as a complement. This justifies the semisimplicity used in the simultaneous action-map argument. [F4, algebra]
 
